-- §13.17 (issue #18): file descriptors. fopen/fwrite/fclose/fread,
-- gated on the (Fopen, H) / (Fwrote, H) / (Fread, H) completion tuples
-- in Global, data pushed and pulled through the §9 bytestring
-- side-table. The file is created next to this program and left
-- behind — open it to see what the machines wrote.
--
-- Every say is gated on the completion tuple of the read that
-- produced its content (§14): a (Fread, H) gate lands atomically with
-- the side-table write, so when the gate is matchable the content is
-- there. An in-transaction `out` (the old (Show,) shape) is NOT such
-- a gate — the §8.2 two-phase split means the read queued next to it
-- has not run yet — so under a shuffled effect queue or concurrent
-- runners (§11.13, the chaos knob) that shape could say before its
-- read landed (unknown bytestring handle, fatal). The second phase
-- joins on the gate AND a (Round,) marker: the marker exists only
-- after the first gate was consumed, so only the SECOND (Fread, Back)
-- can satisfy it.

: fopen Log "files-out.tmp" W
(Fopen, Log) : bytesBind Line "first line from the machines"
(Bytes, Line) : fwrite Log Line
(Fwrote, Log) : [fclose Log; fopen Back "files-out.tmp" R]
-- The first fread pulls the whole file (the read fd is spent — strict
-- hGetContents closes the OS handle as it reads); the second sees the
-- empty remainder.
(Fopen, Back) : fread Back
(Fread, Back) : [say "read back: %b" Back; (Round,); fread Back; die]
(Fread, Back), (Round,) : [say "second fread (fd spent): [%b]" Back; exit 0]
